an adorable robot cannibalizes his peers  babies are produced in questionable ways  an allegory for screen addiction from  one of the biggest media companies in  the world these are the things only  adults notice while watching wall-e  in retrospect it's clear that 2008 was a  simpler time it was only two years after  the documentary and inconvenient truth  began to really spread awareness of the  threat that global warming posed to the  planet and the more broadly sinister  blanket term climate change was hardly  in the news at all the ensuing years  have seen an increasing number of  record-setting temperatures at various  points all over the world with them has  come more frequent wildfires polar  vortexes unusually active hurricane  seasons and countless other disasters  that are all attributable to climate  change wally is almost quaint in  representing the harm that we've done to  the planet our small resolute waste  allocation load lifter earth class robot  has apparently spent centuries packing  refuse into cubes making for a  strikingly apt visual metaphor but even  though we aren't yet piling garbage so  high that it towers over skyscrapers  trash is indeed a big part of the real  climate crisis in reality landfills are  estimated to account for 10 percent of  greenhouse gas emissions by 2025 in  addition to causing a host of other  environmental issues the backdrop of  wall-e was already one of the most  chilling and relevant settings for any  kids movie when it came out and it only  becomes more terrifyingly relevant with  each passing year  even if you can summon the will to set  the reality of climate concerns aside  and just let wally cast its spell on you  the central premise is still almost  unbearably sad there's just no way to  look past the fact that wally is a small  robot whose sole purpose is to condense  waste into neatly stacked cubes he's  been laboring at this job for around 700  years on a deserted planet earth with  only a cockroach and a trailer full of  junk for company the opening scenes of  the movie are set to a jaunty show tune  and they manage to mind some humor out  of wally's confused reactions to  recognizable everyday objects but if you  actually stop and think about this  robot's existence it's a rather modeling  way for a film to begin fortunately  wally only takes us through one day in  the life of its impossibly lonely  protagonist before a mysterious rocket  lands and the real adventure begins but  for the first 10 minutes or so kids and  adults alike are invited to contemplate  solitude and futility in the bleakest  opening in pixar history  [Music]  not only does wally have a premise  that's both horrifically sad and  unnervingly relevant it's also largely  about two robots that only say a half a  dozen words or so between the two of  them when you think about it it's  amazing that audiences were able to  emotionally connect with the story at  all that they did is in large part  thanks to songs from the 1969 movie  musical hello dolly wally has managed to  find a vhs copy of this particular film  that improbably still works and repeated  clips of just two of its songs serve as  emotional shorthand at several different  points the peppy put on your sunday  clothes serves as the innovating ironic  choice to open the movie as we zoom in  on the polluted empty earth it then goes  on to be a theme song for both wally's  curiosity and optimism as well as a  rallying cry for his robot brethren  aboard the axiom spaceship meanwhile it  only takes a moment is the theme song  for wally and eve's romantic connection  there's even a scene set to lavien rose  by louis armstrong who also appeared in  hello dolly thanks to the contributions  of a classic musical alongside thomas  newman's subtle yet wonderful score  revisiting wally as an adult really  makes you consider how much music can  accomplish in a nearly wordless film  in the future presented in wali the  corporation by and large is  indistinguishable from the government  ceo shelby forthright played in a few  scenes by fred willard in live action  speaks from a podium in front of a blue  backdrop that looks a lot like the white  house it's implied that by and large is  solely responsible for operation cleanup  the plan to save earth while humanity is  off-world on luxury space cruise ships  it's something that initially seems  darkly ironic and silly but it has grim  and sinister implications that only get  more uncomfortable for those of us  who've aged through major recessions and  government misconduct over the years  because at b l space is the final  fun tier  you might think that disney who bought  pixar just a couple years before wally  was released wouldn't want to pull the  thread on how giant corporations with  government ties are leading us down a  ruinous environmental path of course  this was before disney went on to  acquire marvel studios lucasfilm and  20th century fox so it wasn't as on the  nose at the time as it is today still  it's more than a little ironic in  retrospect how the general theme of  wally seems to tell us to stop being  such consumers even though the movie is  a commercial product from one of the  biggest companies in human history  another thing that we may have already  forgotten about life in 2008 is that  screens weren't as omnipresent in our  lives as they are now most phones were  still of the flipped variety and plenty  of people still used blackberries  devices then weren't nearly as  entrancing as modern day smartphones  with their endless apps games and  constant demand on our attention netflix  had just barely introduced its streaming  service so youtube was the only real  video streaming option with only a  fraction of the content that it has  today in the years since streaming has  become ubiquitous and if we're not  careful about our screen time habits  then we'll find ourselves in an  existence that's basically a  planet-sized version of the axiom most  chillingly especially after the mass  shift to remote work during the covet-19  pandemic wall-e's human characters are  seen using a zoom-like interface in  their hoverchair displays to talk to  each other instead of just getting up  and having face-to-face interactions  meanwhile in the real world we've got  screens in our cars screens on our  appliances and screens on our wrist  watches devices are getting so smart  that it will be a great challenge for  modern humanity to remember that we're  on a planet and not a spaceship thus  wally serves as a helpful cautionary  tale to remind us that we can't  completely rely on this so-called smart  technology  my nephew bought me a smartphone for  chris if it's so smart why can't i get a  driver's license it's only through  wally's chaotic influence that we see  two humans on the axiom mary and john  start to interact with each other and  accidentally hold hands the novelty of  it shines on their delighted bashful  faces for adult viewers however an  unsettling thought comes crashing in to  undercut it if that's where people are  in the future in terms of potential  relationships then how have new babies  been born on the axiom for the last 700  years we distinctly see a group of  toddlers being taught by robots in a  daycare earlier in the film but  considering that we also see the captain  of the axiom struggling to walk in a  later scene it's a safe bet that nobody  on the ship is doing anything much more  strenuous than that the implication is  obviously that somewhere on the ship  there's a place where humans are grown  instead of born perhaps matrix style  just how the machines on the ship make  this happen we can only imagine wally is  largely an allegory and there are lots  of things we don't learn about in terms  of day-to-day life on the axiom but the  fact that we specifically get to see a  group of babies makes thoughts of basic  reproduction unavoidable and unavoidably  unsettling  wally strikes a carefully calculated  balance viewers need to identify with  the main robot so that they're not  thinking too hard about environmental  strife or the end of society it works  remarkably well except for how  cavalierly wally scavenges parts from  dead robot that look exactly like  himself first he pauses by the remains  of another wall-e unit to steal its  tread afterward when eve damages one of  his eyes he casually reveals that he has  an entire shelf of discarded wally parts  to choose from to replace it for adult  viewers this can result in a strange  cognitive dissonance in the movie's  emotional climax wally has been so badly  damaged that eve has to replace several  more parts and an internal circuit board  we're left in doubt for a moment that  wally even remembers her as he may be a  sort of undead frankenstein's monster of  other dead wallies with no memory  everything ultimately turns out okay but  only if you're willing to ignore wally's  robot cannibalism  there's so much that wally doesn't want  you to think too hard about that it's  hard to come away with a clear moral  lesson children's movies aren't  obligated to have one of course but they  almost always offer some sort of message  in the vein of just be yourself wally  presents a vague theme about the  difference between surviving and living  but it can be hard to really connect it  to anything particularly concrete  obviously we shouldn't let the planet  drown in garbage or the atmosphere  become toxic but what does wally suggest  we do to avoid it the damage from  climate change is so far reaching that  it's baffling to know where to start as  an individual to make a difference other  than a vague resolution to avoid excess  screen time and not be a passenger on  the proverbial axiom of the internet  there's not much that wally leaves you  with other than a warm feeling for cute  robots that's great and all but it won't  get all that excess carbon dioxide out  of the air  the technology depicted in wall-e was  all apparently made around the year 2100  when earth was becoming uninhabitable it  includes spaceships that are still  running perfectly after several hundred  years food replicators that feed the  entire ship's population to excess an  endless array of robots and hyperspace  technology that gets the axiom back to  earth in a matter of seconds so with all  this technology at its disposal how can  it be that the best plan that humanity's  corporate overlords could conceive of  was to leave a bunch of trash scooping  robots behind and try to wait the crisis  out on a cruise why not use the  hyperspace tech to try to find other  planets to colonize why aren't there  robots designed to scrub carbon from the  atmosphere why not shoot all the garbage  on earth into the sun perhaps there's an  intentionally cynical message and by and  large's plan to sell humans on a cruise  to distract them from the devastation  that their own consumerism has wrought  but you would think that finding a more  efficient solution would be good for the  company's bottom line as well  the thing that makes wally great in the  midst of all of its depressingly  realistic concerns about our present and  future is the fact that it barely  actually talks about those concerns at  all the first half is basically a silent  comedy as we watch our plucky hero fill  the time as best he can then it becomes  a breathless high-flying adventure when  eve shows up to shake him from his  routine the music score and beautiful  animation make wally a singular film  there's nothing else like it after  repeated viewings you'll probably start  to recognize every little mumble and  jittery noise that wally makes as an  extension of his personality  dialogue forms the backbone of most  movies it's how characters deliver key  exposition and reveal their motivations  directly or indirectly so by largely  foregoing spoken words wally achieves a  once in a century feat of storytelling  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

